<section xml:id="sec-rank-thm">
  <title>The Rank Theorem</title>

  <objectives>
    <ol>
      <li><p>Learn to understand and use the rank theorem.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Picture:</em> the rank theorem.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Theorem:</em> rank theorem.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Vocabulary words:</em> <term>rank</term>, <term>nullity</term>.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </objectives>

  <p>
    In this section we present the rank theorem, which is the culmination of all of the work we have done so far.
  </p>

    <p>
      The reader may have observed a relationship between the column space and the null space of a matrix.  In <xref ref="subspaces-col-nul-1"/>, the column space and the null space of a <m>3\times 2</m> matrix are both lines, in <m>\R^2</m> and <m>\R^3</m>, respectively:
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
